s when black  patients see  black doctors motivation is not enough  it's dr william mcdade's job to help  more minorities  make it in medicine as the head of  diversity for the national committee  that oversees medical training  there are economic disadvantages that  have to be overcome there are  educational deficits that have to be  overcome  and a structural racism that exists  within society that actually  carries into medicine as well he says  children of color  also need to see doctors who look like  them something dr palmer and his brother  tim  did not have back in akron ohio where we  grew up at  no one's a doctor no one's a lawyer no  one's  a judge their parents raised five kids  in a two-bedroom house  this is the home that we grew up in it's  all torn down now  as his mother amy recalled i had to  teach my kids not to pick up syringes  when they saw him on the sidewalk  and that is not an exaggeration but like  many  low-income kids of color he lacked  resources and mentorship  until his college professor ann capilla  gave him textbooks and confidence dr  palmer  we arranged a reunion more than a decade  after a pivotal meeting that's the first  time i've called you that  i was probably expecting her to say it's  not something that's going to happen for  you  but she said and i'll never forget this  she said  of course i think you can do it how  important is it to hear someone say  you can do it that moment is the reason  why i'm  actually standing here with her support  he got into  medical school but four years later no  residency program  accepted him you have a medical degree  correct without  anywhere to go correct dr mcdade says  medical students of color  often struggle with isolation imposter  syndrome and standardized testing  and are more likely than whites to not  get into residency or not complete  residency training  but dr palmer tried again and  northwestern  said yes you look great my name is aaron  palmer i used to live in that blue house  over here  i'm down in chicago now if you're a  surgeon yeah operate on the brain it's  fine  what would your 10 year old self think  of someone like you  poor little 10 year old aaron would his  mind would be blown  now he's the role model his neighborhood  never had  it's a pleasure thank you  and dr palmer is paying it forward he  mentors younger students  and is researching racial disparities  but make no mistake the state of  diversity and medicine  is dire the percentage of black and  latinx doctors  has hardly budged in the last 15 years  vlad a mentor is someone who helps you  find the hope within yourself adriana  thank you so much what a wonderful story
 

